// The two region-A routes as the generator measures them, over the
// domain the two tables cover - every order's own pieces, from zero to
// kX0: the stored coefficients each evaluates and the worst
// |F_n - fit| each reaches, swept on a stride-1 grid of each piece's own
// interval, in the kernel's double arithmetic and against the reference
// the fits are validated against. A swept maximum on a finite grid, not
// a bound: the bar both routes are certified against is kRegionAFitBar.
// The rational route's selector takes over at kRatARouteLo - the lowest
// of region A's per-order ends - and per order from that order's own
// end, which is where the lane stops reading the order from its own fit
// and documents the band's 3e-14 rather than the per-order 1e-15. Below
// such an end the lane is documented at 1e-15, which these pieces do not
// hold. The rational pieces were accepted below the bar rather than at
// it, so the bar survives a certifying sweep on a different grid.
inline constexpr double kRegionAFitBar = 2.99999999999999984e-14;
inline constexpr int kRegionAFitChebStored = 1320;
inline constexpr double kRegionAFitChebDelivered = 1.73613094673444924e-16;
inline constexpr int kRegionAFitRatStored = 891;
inline constexpr double kRegionAFitRatDelivered = 2.45899136175476671e-14;

// The evaluation schemes, one row per (scheme, stored fit): the
// degree and stored count the fit uses, and the bound each scheme is
// held to on it against the 60-digit reference, in each multiply-add
// route. scheme 0 = split Clenshaw, 1 = Horner; lane 0 = region A
// (kCoeffs), 1 = region B (kBcoeffs), 2 = the extended band
// (kExtendedBcoeffs). Each bound is the worst error a sweep over the
// fit's own interval reached, rounded up to the next power of two, so
// it is a bound and not the sweep's reading.
struct SchemeRow {
    int scheme, lane, region, deg, stored;
    double fused, separate;
};

